WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.988
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.474 --> 00:00:08.080
YANDU OPPACHER:
Hi, good afternoon.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.080
I'm Yandu.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.310
I'm with Shopify.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:13.185
I'm a director on the Data
Platform Engineering Team.

00:00:13.185 --> 00:00:14.560
And earlier this
year, we started

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.210
migrating our data center
from on-prem in Chicago

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.500
up to Google Cloud.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.310
Shopify is the leading
cloud-based commerce platform.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.720
We started in 2012.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.840
We have about 600,000
users, active merchants,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.670
and spread around 175 countries.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:40.400
And together, they've sold $63
billion worth of merchandise.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.750
So that's really
Shopify by the numbers.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:48.830
But what Shopify tries to
do is also make a place

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.860
where an individual brand can--

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.410
an individual merchant
can build its brand,

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:05.170
and build their brand,
and really find out

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.800
their unique voice and be
able to bring that to market.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.550
And we try to support
them through our customer

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:17.170
support, our ecosystem
of apps and developers,

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.390
and really just try to
make sure that they've got

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:21.640
every advantage that they can.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.490
So this is why we're here.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.530
My senior VP of Data
in the fall of 2016

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.460
said, all data processing
needs to be in the cloud by Q4.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.010
Fortunately, he
didn't say what year.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:43.070
But it still had us
thinking and worrying.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.410
And so that's what he said.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.102
Implicitly, he also said we
can't have any SLO violations.

00:01:50.102 --> 00:01:52.310
Our customers still need to
be able to get their data

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.190
insights.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.280
And the business
Shopify needs to be

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.070
able to answer the questions
it has, and also no data

00:01:58.070 --> 00:01:59.450
disruptions.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.460
So we couldn't lose data,
we couldn't corrupt it

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.310
during this migration.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.650
So we managed to do it.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.320
And I'm really excited
to tell you about it.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.770
But first, I want to talk
a little bit about the data

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.790
platform as it was in the
DC and how it grew up.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.310
So this is going to be
really brief introduction

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.650
to our infrastructure.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.320
So this is how it started.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.630
These servers arrived
the same week I did.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.690
And my bootstrapping
onboarding task

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:31.310
was to bootstrap these nodes.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.590
Took me two days to
realize that our HDFS nodes

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.740
didn't have any
hard drives in them.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.800
Once we figured out why
we couldn't install an OS,

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.810
everything went smoothly.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.030
So from there, once we had
the machines up and running,

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.390
we installed HDFS as our
data lake for file storage,

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:54.600
YARN for our compute and
running all of our jobs.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.500
The jobs primarily are Spark.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.380
And that's where all
of our ETL does happen.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.900
It's modeling the data.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.720
And then finally,
about two years ago we

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.240
introduced Presto
as our query engine

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:09.920
so that we could
make all of HDFS

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.960
data available and
queryable via SQL

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.470
to our analysts and
the rest of Shopify.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.350
So final state, we had
11 petabytes of storage.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.520
By the time we migrated, we were
using about nine and 1/2 of it.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.730
Between Presto and YARN, we
had around 250 compute nodes.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:34.090
We added 80 terabytes
of data per day.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:34.980
This is net new.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.480
The amount that we
actually produced

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.480
was a lot higher,
around 250 terabytes.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.830
But that was with our
lifecycle management.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.920
Really net new was
just 80 terabytes.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.670
And then finally we run
25,000 ETL jobs per day

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.410
on that nine petabytes of data.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.550
So from pretty
humble beginnings,

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.030
we grew to a pretty big size.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.250
And we were starting
to feel the strain

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:05.050
of managing this infrastructure
with a small team.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.460
We went from buying
servers, to buying racks,

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:08.860
to building out data cages.

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:18.399
So what does it do?

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:21.170
Basically this, just
lots and lots of ETL.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.740
This is where people do their
exploration, their modeling,

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.660
where we build
data insights both

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.220
for our internal customers,
but also for our merchants.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:33.610
So it breaks down the way
we think of it logically

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.670
into data acquisition, which
is how the data comes in.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:40.210
This is data pulled in from
our MySQL shards, and also

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:44.800
our Kafka Message Bus,
plus third-party services

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.810
like GitHub or ZenHub.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.750
And then once the data lands,
data processing takes over.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.450
This is the majority
of the Spark jobs

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:55.450
that then take this data,
model it, and build out

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:58.810
of the data artifacts
and data assets

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:00.860
that we use to
power our business,

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.800
but also help our merchants.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.200
And then finally, we've got
data discovery and access.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.010
And this is really how
the data assets make it

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:10.790
back to our customers.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.300
And it's mainly
through Tableau or Mode

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.640
for exploration and reporting.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.640
And then we've got Redshift
as an analytic database,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:24.954
and then also Presto for
querying and exploration.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:32.030
That's where we
were January 2018.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.340
And then we found
out our data center

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:36.610
and contracts were up in July.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.520
So we had six months.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.120
So we had roughly five
infrastructure engineers,

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.660
15 to 20 data engineers,
and six months.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:52.460
So for us, we really had
to focus on time to cloud.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:55.290
And we made a bunch of
decisions around that

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:58.050
to make sure that we could
get out of the data center

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.740
as quickly as possible.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:04.550
So we were optimizing
for speed and trying

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.120
to also not impact our
customers, like the data

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:12.320
scientists, and really
try to replace what we had

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.830
and make sure that it's as much
of an implementation detail

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:16.400
as possible.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:22.555
So to do that, what we
did was we moved to--

00:06:22.555 --> 00:06:24.180
we really just wanted
to lift-and-shift

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:25.590
our infrastructure.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.317
We understood HDFS,
YARN, and Spark.

00:06:28.317 --> 00:06:30.900
And this is also what all of our
customers were familiar with.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.760
We didn't want to
burden them with having

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.670
to port all of their models
and all of their jobs

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:41.900
over if we could just move it
up to GCP without them seeing

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.780
or having to worry about it.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.200
But as much as we didn't
want to use new services,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.470
we also wanted to be
pragmatic and use services

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.010
where it made sense.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.330
So for us, there was
an operational burden

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.159
running things like HDFS.

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:00.200
So if we could shift that
operational burden over

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.160
to Google, we did it.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.770
And then, we also knew
that we needed to get out

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:11.730
of the DC quickly and into GCP.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.200
And we knew it wasn't
going to be perfect,

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.650
but we just needed
to do his job and be

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:19.470
able to get all of our
data assets rolling again.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:23.020
So, we knew that there
was lots of optimizations

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.020
we could do once we had
the flexibility of being

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.880
in Google Cloud.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.170
So we wanted to-- but none
of those optimizations

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:33.510
would actually help us
get out of the DC faster.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.360
So things we could do with
compute, preemptible VMs,

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:41.051
running clusters that are
specific to the workload,

00:07:41.051 --> 00:07:43.050
those sorts of things are
great, but again, they

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:47.220
wouldn't help us move
out of the DC faster.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.010
So the first piece to
move was data discovery.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.280
This is like starting
with dessert first,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.310
but there's a
reason we did this.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:59.620
So just as a reminder,
data discovery,

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.770
this is really the
portal into the data

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:06.840
warehouse that we gave all of
our customers within Shopify.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.810
So this is Presto
for querying and also

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:14.660
Tableau and Mode for reporting.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.080
So these are the
reasons why we chose

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.215
to do the data discovery first.

00:08:21.215 --> 00:08:22.590
One of the big
ones is that there

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:25.100
is no data set dependencies.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.670
Really, all the consumers
wanted was the latest data.

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:31.310
We didn't have to worry about
operational interdependencies

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.280
to actually generate this data.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:39.770
Also, we knew we were going to
have to move that three and 1/2

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.609
petabytes of data up to GCP.

00:08:42.609 --> 00:08:45.410
So we we're going to have to
get good at moving data anyways,

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:50.030
so we might as well do it
with a slightly lower load.

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:54.200
And then it was also the
one feeling the most pain.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.670
Presto usage took
off like wildfire.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.670
We started with four
nodes, and had to scale out

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:00.840
to 60 within a year.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:04.220
And it runs 25,000
queries per day

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.920
across the three and 1/2
petabytes of data that we have.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:21.670
When Presto really is our
query engine and SQL engine

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.530
to all of the data in HDFS--

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.360
and when we look
at our data lake,

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:28.150
we think of it in two ways.

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:30.310
We've got the raw
data, which is what

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:31.510
data acquisition brings in.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.240
This is really just
operationalized data

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.130
that we bring in,
consolidate, and have

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:37.296
a historical record of.

00:09:37.296 --> 00:09:38.920
And then there's the
model data assets,

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:45.070
which are cleaned and
built by our ETL framework.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:48.990
So this is what it looked
like in the data center.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.705
Basically we had
HDFS and Presto.

00:09:51.705 --> 00:09:54.817
Redshift was our first
analytic database.

00:09:54.817 --> 00:09:56.400
And it only contained
front room data.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:04.810
So once front room data
was built via the ETL,

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:06.797
we'd package it up,
we'd ship it up to S3,

00:10:06.797 --> 00:10:08.630
load it into Redshift,
and then people could

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.430
start reporting off of it.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:13.430
Presto had access to
all of the data in HDFS.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.050
And that's where
it queried from.

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.180
So all it needed to do
is really a metadata.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:19.524
We just say this
is the latest data.

00:10:19.524 --> 00:10:20.690
You can start querying that.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:27.050
This is the pipeline that we
had to build for the migration.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:28.630
So the good news
is that we already

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:31.270
knew how to move
quite a bit of data

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:34.240
because we already moved
the data from HDFS to S3

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:36.100
for front room data.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:38.620
And then we added
this new pipeline

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:42.647
to move the front room data
from HDFS up to Presto.

00:10:42.647 --> 00:10:44.230
The one part that
we didn't quite have

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:46.120
yet was for the raw data.

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.960
And this is where the majority
of that 250 terabytes that we

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:51.966
build everyday goes.

00:10:51.966 --> 00:10:53.590
So we had to work
with our network team

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.830
to make sure that we didn't
DDoS ourselves going with all

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:58.840
this data out the front door.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:04.060
So our network team looked
at the amount of data

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:06.670
we wanted to move
and also the latency.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:09.090
And this was an
important piece too

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.320
was that we had to
go and understand

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:15.380
what our customers wanted
in terms of latency.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:19.610
For them, new data is generated
around every four to eight

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:20.720
hours.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.650
So an extra 15 to 20
minutes of needing to wait

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:26.150
for the freshest data was OK.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:32.150
No relevancy would be lost
by this lower latency.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.100
So after discussing,
understanding

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:37.292
that the latency concerns
that we had, how much data

00:11:37.292 --> 00:11:38.750
we wanted to move,
and also wanting

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:40.880
a little bit of head
room for growth,

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:45.830
we decided we got four
40-gigabit direct connects

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:49.940
with Google and made sure that
all data coming from our data

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:53.510
warehouse was segregated and ran
specifically over those links

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:56.620
so that we wouldn't impact
anything else in the data

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:57.120
center.

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:02.610
And moving this
data, we had already

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.760
gotten good at being
as part of our workload

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.050
uploading the front room assets.

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:12.750
And now we also needed to
upload the back room assets

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:13.960
and register them.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:15.990
But this was just another
step in the workflow

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:17.800
that we already
understood and knew well.

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:24.820
So for part of our migration,
we Dockerized Presto.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.220
One of the reasons we
did this was in the DC,

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.950
we maintained everything
with Chef, our configuration

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:31.196
management.

00:12:31.196 --> 00:12:33.070
But that wasn't going
to be maintained once--

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:34.695
Chef itself wasn't
going to actually be

00:12:34.695 --> 00:12:36.505
supported once we left the DC.

00:12:36.505 --> 00:12:39.130
So we needed to
move away from it.

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:43.910
And we also wanted to have
immutable infrastructure.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.560
So this is the format
that we came up with.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:47.810
So we have Presto dockerized.

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:50.540
It's pretty simple and
an easy one to dockerize.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.780
And then we also dockerized all
of our monitoring and logging

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:55.540
tooling.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.470
All of these run as system
D unit file-- or sorry,

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:00.600
unit services.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.980
And we ran on
container optimized OS.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:09.400
We chose that because
it's optimized for Docker.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.800
And we also didn't have
to worry about somebody

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:13.540
going onto an Ubuntu
image or something,

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:15.289
and running scripts,
and installing stuff,

00:13:15.289 --> 00:13:17.770
and slowly the clusters
started to drift.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:19.999
We already had a
very good pipeline

00:13:19.999 --> 00:13:22.290
for version control and
building out Docker containers.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:23.920
So we just jumped on that.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:30.560
So once we had the
nodes, we started

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.500
using Google Deployment
Manager to actually

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:35.310
be able to deploy the clusters.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:39.270
And this gave us the
ability to quickly iterate

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:41.840
because we didn't have to slowly
stand everything up and make

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.400
sure the config
files were there.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:48.140
We could just lean on Google
Deployment Manager for that.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:51.240
Also, Presto, it's
pretty simple.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.980
There's a coordinator and
workers to do the work.

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:58.850
And this gave us a easy
way to start understanding

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:02.540
how Deployment Manager works
and how to actually build out

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:04.967
more complicated clusters.

00:14:04.967 --> 00:14:06.800
And we used instance
groups so that we could

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:11.010
scale at the workers on demand.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:12.980
So once we had the
cluster, and everything up,

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.289
and we're moving
the data, we started

00:14:15.289 --> 00:14:16.580
to run in parallel with the DC.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:19.850
We made sure that we
could stage the data,

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:22.880
that it was consolidated.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.730
And it was reconciled to make
sure that the data we thought

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:27.860
was there is actually
the data that was there.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:32.040
And we opened it up for
beta to some data scientists

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.900
who were super eager to
go and start using it

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:40.180
because they could get away from
the current cluster, which was

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:41.470
overloaded with too much work.

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:48.910
So after we reconciled, made
sure the data was there,

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:51.280
and we're comfortable with
how we were deploying it,

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:53.470
and felt it was
ready for production,

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:55.210
we flipped the switch.

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:58.697
So until now, the DC
Presto was still running.

00:14:58.697 --> 00:15:00.280
And it was the single
source of truth.

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:01.696
When we were ready,
we switched it

00:15:01.696 --> 00:15:05.170
over so that we switched
all the reporting

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:07.420
to start going against GCP.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:14.030
This was in late Q1
that we switched over.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:17.290
So once we had the
Presto up and running,

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:21.520
we also wanted to think
about security a bit more.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:27.100
So in the DC, we secured
it with VPN at the edge.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:30.000
But what we lost there was
some auditability because

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.430
without a plug-in, Presto
just allows anybody

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:38.010
to put whatever username that
they want into the user field.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:41.970
So we knew the people that
were using it, so it was OK.

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:44.190
And there is trust
within the data org.

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:46.530
But we wanted to open
it up to more than that.

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:48.410
And so what we
did was we created

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:52.290
an OAuth proxy, which is similar
to the Identity-Aware proxy.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:54.600
And basically what
happens is a user

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:57.990
will log in via this proxy
in their web browser,

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:00.290
follow the OAuth flow
that they're used to.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:05.030
We get back a signed
token from Google.

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:10.910
Then the user can connect to
the client and their proxy.

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:15.380
And that client will proxy their
request along with the token

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:17.030
up to the server.

00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:20.360
Once it's on the server, we
now have the signed JWT token

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:22.880
that we know exactly who it is.

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:26.110
And then we can set the username
before it goes into Presto.

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:28.780
So this gave us
end-to-end auditability

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:31.940
on all of our queries.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:34.480
So that was moving Presto.

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:36.550
So we started with
the end of the meal.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:39.040
And now we're going to actually,
I guess, set the table.

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:47.090
So again, this is how all of the
data enters our data warehouse.

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:53.100
We pull it in from MySQL
shards, from Kafka,

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:57.420
and from third-party
sources like GitHub.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:59.880
It's really the only
place where we've

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:03.190
got operational
snapshots of the data.

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:05.399
And this is the base
of all of our ETLs.

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:10.440
So Speedboat is the
tool that we built

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:16.690
to run our data acquisition.

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:23.339
It's written up in this unholy
pairing of JRuby and Scala.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:25.589
It's a basic change
data capture.

00:17:25.589 --> 00:17:27.359
And it produces,
again, full snapshots

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:29.460
of the operational models.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:37.100
And the way Shopify core
is running in our DC

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:38.030
is we're sharded.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:41.960
So that means we've scaled
all the shops horizontally.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:45.230
And the data warehouse
is really the only place

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:49.950
you can get a consolidated
view across all of Shopify.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:52.750
So Speedboat has
two main phases.

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:54.082
One is the extract phase.

00:17:54.082 --> 00:17:56.040
This is where we actually
pull in the new data.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:58.350
And this is basically
like a select star

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:01.380
where updated at is
greater than whatever.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.420
So we get back all
the data that's

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:05.910
changed since the
last time we did this.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.320
And then we fold that
into an existing snapshot

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:11.490
to produce the new operational
table at that time.

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:21.600
So for moving to the
cloud, we had to move to--

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.404
we started leveraging
some managed services.

00:18:24.404 --> 00:18:26.070
And this part was
really good because it

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:29.040
allowed another team, not
just the infrastructure team,

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.470
to actually be able to
own their infrastructure.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:35.120
They decided to
use Google Cloud--

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:40.130
or sorry, Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:47.510
as the extractor and colocated
that with Shopify core

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:50.150
to be able to access
all the databases.

00:18:50.150 --> 00:18:53.160
They also use Dataproc to be
able to build these snapshots.

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:56.150
This was all built on
top of Spark and Scala.

00:18:56.150 --> 00:19:01.070
And then we used Google Cloud
Storage for our storage engine,

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:04.180
our storage layer.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:05.920
So this is how it looked.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:10.720
And basically, Speedboat
would run its extracts

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:11.980
from all the different shards.

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:14.290
It would land those
extracts onto GCS.

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:17.260
And then Dataproc would pick
it up, fold the new changes

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:20.320
into the existing snapshot,
and put it back down on GCS.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:22.940
And that's how we got the
root for all of our modeling.

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.320
So this is the piece
that took the longest.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:31.810
And this is the big hairy
beast that nobody really

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:33.760
wanted to tackle.

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:35.860
This is the heart of
our data warehouse.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.650
And this is where all of
the raw data gets refined

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:44.630
and where we build
out the data assets.

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:46.050
It's insanely complex.

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:48.770
So this is about a third
of our dependency graph.

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:57.060
That's 3,500 individual jobs
which run 25,000 times a day.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.580
So in the DC, I've
mentioned this

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:05.940
before, we have a
YARN cluster running

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:10.650
on top of HDFS as our data lake
and PySpark as the main driver

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:14.210
for all of our data and ETLing.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:23.270
So at Shopify, we started using
Spark and PySpark specifically

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:27.980
around the latter part of
2013 and into early 2014.

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:30.320
And so we've got a
lot of legacy baggage

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:32.360
that came along with that.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:36.560
One of them was that we had
to be able to distribute

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:38.500
our work across the cluster.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.300
We couldn't just run
it on a single node.

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:45.840
And this was before YARN cluster
mode was fully available.

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:47.810
So what we had to do was
dockerize our driver,

00:20:47.810 --> 00:20:49.580
make sure that
everything was there,

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:50.750
all the native libraries.

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:53.090
And then we could actually
run that Docker driver

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:55.580
anywhere in the cluster
to spin up a new YARN job.

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:59.140
And we weren't confined
to a single box.

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:01.040
And again, because
of PySpark, there

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:04.380
were a lot of native libraries
that we had to deal with.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:11.360
So NumPy, SymPy, SciPy, these
were all native libraries

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:14.120
that we had to compile and run.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:19.070
And it took about 40 minutes
to bootstrap a new node

00:21:19.070 --> 00:21:20.560
with these dependencies.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:26.390
So how did we migrate
this to the cloud?

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:33.120
So again, looking
at time to cloud,

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:37.070
we had to make a couple
of choices up front.

00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:39.650
And one of the major
architectural changes

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:45.740
we had made was going
from HDFS to GCS.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:50.810
And this was, again, trying
to deal with context-free

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:53.090
versus context-full,
so something

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:57.450
where we didn't have any
competitive advantage

00:21:57.450 --> 00:22:00.320
of running HDFS ourselves.

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:07.230
And also it's cost prohibitive
in GCP to run HDFS.

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:11.780
So we decided to move
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:14.660
There was also the added benefit
the Google Cloud Storage's API

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:18.320
is compatible with
HDFS's file system API.

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:20.570
So it was a drop in replacement.

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:24.510
But GCS isn't a file system.

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:27.110
And this caused
us a lot of grief.

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:29.340
It's similar if
you're driving a car

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:31.050
with an automatic
transmission, and then

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:34.105
you swap out and give it
a manual transmission.

00:22:34.105 --> 00:22:35.730
You're still going
to be able to drive,

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:37.105
but now there's
a lot more things

00:22:37.105 --> 00:22:39.510
that you have to worry about.

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:42.780
And for us, there were a lot
of built-in assumptions with

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:45.530
the way that our framework--

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:50.900
our four ETLs--
did its job, mainly

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:53.240
about atomic directory moves.

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:55.350
That doesn't happen anymore.

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:57.740
So what we had to do
was add sentinel files

00:22:57.740 --> 00:22:59.489
to ensure that we
weren't picking up

00:22:59.489 --> 00:23:00.530
partially processed data.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:07.800
So again, we took
the same approach.

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:10.710
We had Chef running in GCP--

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:11.610
or sorry, in our DC.

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:16.930
And it had grown organically.

00:23:16.930 --> 00:23:19.100
The configuration management
for this, for Hadoop,

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:20.660
had grown organically.

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:22.250
I tried for two
months to refactor it,

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:23.840
but then rage quit.

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:27.290
So, we didn't want to bring
this ball of YARN and this mess

00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:28.860
up with us to GCP.

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:31.940
So again, we looked at
dockerizing everything.

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:40.940
This was a more complicated
deployment than Presto.

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:43.760
But we had already understood
some of the best practices

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:45.870
from going through
the Presto migration.

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:49.160
So we again used GDM for
deploying and managing

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:51.200
our deployments.

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:56.080
And then we decided to use
standard Google Compute Engine

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:58.150
VMs to deploy onto.

00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:01.330
So we desperately
wanted to use Dataproc.

00:24:01.330 --> 00:24:04.810
But we couldn't
ever get the boot

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:09.737
up time for a Dataproc cluster
to be under 30 minutes.

00:24:09.737 --> 00:24:12.070
And that's because of all
these native libraries that we

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:13.840
had to compile and build up.

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:17.620
So there was no way of
actually amortizing the cost.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:21.520
We wanted to pay that
cost of building once

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:24.440
and then be able
to deploy quickly.

00:24:24.440 --> 00:24:26.950
So what we did was
we leaned on the fact

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:28.960
that we had already
dockerized and paid

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:34.720
the cost for the drivers
for all of our ETL jobs.

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.400
Then all we needed
to do was really

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:40.680
overlay the node manager and
resource manager libraries

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:42.100
over top.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:44.280
And so now we could go
down from 40 minutes

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:47.620
to spin up a new node
down to two to three.

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:51.970
So this is what
we ended up with.

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:58.840
Again, it's very similar
to the Presto nodes.

00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:02.330
We have the node manager
running all the jobs.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:05.050
All of our logging
and monitoring

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:08.831
are also Docker containers built
up on container-optimized OS.

00:25:08.831 --> 00:25:10.330
The one thing that
we had to change,

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:15.040
though, was the way that
we launch ETL jobs because

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:16.510
of the Docker container.

00:25:16.510 --> 00:25:18.640
We had to actually
mount the Doctor socket

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:21.460
into the node manager
so when it spun it up,

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:27.560
it would use the VM's Docker
Daemon to actually spun

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:30.070
these jobs.

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:32.482
We had some issues too
where now, all of a sudden,

00:25:32.482 --> 00:25:34.690
if a node manager failed
because they weren't managed

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:37.930
by the system D service, they
would keep running headless

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:40.700
and potentially cause
some data corruption.

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:44.830
So we had to add in some hooks
into the system D unit files

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:48.010
to make sure that if the
node manager ever shut down,

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:51.421
we'd go and reap all of
the Docker containers.

00:25:55.660 --> 00:25:58.940
So as our deployments
became more complex,

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:00.670
and we deployed
to more projects,

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:03.140
and we also had more
services coming online,

00:26:03.140 --> 00:26:05.530
we invested in
building something

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:08.170
we call Data Deployment
Manager, which is really

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:11.050
just a wrap around GDM
to enforce our best

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:17.690
practices around how we want
to package and deploy versions

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:18.590
or deployments.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:22.040
And it also makes other
teams more autonomous.

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:23.960
Because now instead
of having to make sure

00:26:23.960 --> 00:26:28.070
that they configure the
GDM deploy exactly the way

00:26:28.070 --> 00:26:30.560
we want to, we can
automate this process,

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:33.230
make sure that they're
running in the latest version,

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:35.560
and also have the latest
version of the configs.

00:26:41.950 --> 00:26:45.230
So how we manage
our configs, this

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:50.660
is something that we built in
addition to the Data Deployment

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:51.280
Manager.

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:52.610
And this is Configurator.

00:26:52.610 --> 00:26:56.180
It's a system to manage
and deploy application

00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:57.120
across our clusters.

00:26:57.120 --> 00:27:00.680
It works by keeping versioned
configuration files and source

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:03.890
control and having a
sidecar container run

00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:06.720
on each of the VMs.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:11.310
At startup, the sidecar is
the first one to start up.

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:13.650
It will pull down all the
different config files

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:17.370
and runtime information that we
can get from the Metadata API.

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:19.500
And using a
templating library, we

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:21.510
can stitch these all
together and put them

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:24.480
into a mounted
Docker volume, which

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:28.050
then all the other services
on that node can pull off of.

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:31.330
So they don't
actually need to know

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.150
what the latest
config versions are

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:35.560
or how to stitch them together.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:37.562
They just need to know
which ones they want,

00:27:37.562 --> 00:27:39.520
pull them down, and put
them in the right place

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:41.080
before they start up.

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:43.300
So this allowed us
to also separate

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:45.460
concerns of building
the Docker containers

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:48.190
and deploying the
configurations.

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:50.890
And the nice thing too
here is that we can now

00:27:50.890 --> 00:27:52.720
rollout config
changes without having

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:54.740
to bounce the entire cluster.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:57.700
The Configurator
will pull it down

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:02.730
and build out the latest
version of the configs,

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:06.700
and then make sure that
the services pick them up.

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:14.390
So, this is how we
build out the node

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:17.030
and we built out the
deployment and made

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:20.030
sure that we can do the
configuration properly.

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:21.890
So once we were ready for the--

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:25.350
so our migration plan,
once we had that,

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:27.950
it was really just get
the infrastructure ready.

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:31.490
So the end piece
that you see here

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:34.504
with Configurator and our
Data Deployment Manager,

00:28:34.504 --> 00:28:36.920
that was something that evolved
over the entire migration,

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:38.990
and is still evolving.

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:41.270
What we did and what we
wanted to make sure of

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:44.120
is because of the
tight time deadlines

00:28:44.120 --> 00:28:46.910
that we weren't bottlenecking
anybody above us

00:28:46.910 --> 00:28:50.760
from running tests or
anything like that.

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:55.350
So basically, we lied,
cheated, and stole

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:58.500
to get our first cluster up
to make sure that people could

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:00.600
start running some smoke tests.

00:29:00.600 --> 00:29:03.930
And then as we saw where the
bottlenecks were and issues

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.120
were, we refined our
process and made sure

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:10.790
that it was repeatable,
faster and faster.

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:13.880
Once we had some infrastructure
ready, what we wanted to do

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:16.490
was fire some tracer
bullets to make sure

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:19.340
that the end-to-end
workflow worked as expected,

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:21.140
that there weren't
any huge performance

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:24.140
issues, or networking issues,
or security issues that we just

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:27.020
hadn't thought of.

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:28.460
We did this before
we had to worry

00:29:28.460 --> 00:29:30.980
about that huge dependency
graph I showed before.

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:34.070
And it was really just a
one end-to-end workflow

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:37.150
with no dependencies.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:40.390
So once we were comfortable
that we understood

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:43.780
how we could run one
job from end to end,

00:29:43.780 --> 00:29:46.770
we needed to look at
how to stage the data

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:53.120
and move all of the data
from the DC up to GCP.

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:55.790
And this was so that we could
start running essentially

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:59.780
a full production workload
once we were confident

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:07.440
that at least all the tooling
was there to be able to run it.

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:09.890
So once we had staged
the data and started

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:11.780
to spin up our
production workload,

00:30:11.780 --> 00:30:13.900
we ran in parallel
with the DC again.

00:30:13.900 --> 00:30:18.260
And this was to make sure that
we could reconcile the data.

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:23.965
And this is also a place
where we made some decisions.

00:30:23.965 --> 00:30:26.410
So before the data
acquisition layer

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:29.050
I was talking about how
there is an extract phase,

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:30.929
and that is actually--

00:30:30.929 --> 00:30:32.470
depending on when
you do the extract,

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:34.180
you might get different data.

00:30:34.180 --> 00:30:37.690
And so this is something where
we now have the potential

00:30:37.690 --> 00:30:39.640
to add a little bit of
wobble into the root

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:42.040
of our tree, which
just gets echoed

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:44.870
throughout the entire process.

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:47.500
So we wouldn't have had
any hope of properly

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:51.200
reconciling the data if we
introduced noise at the root.

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:54.790
So what we did was we
actually left the extracts

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:57.740
running in the DC even when
we were running in parallel.

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:01.660
And then we push up
those extracts to GCP

00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:02.920
and then run the snapshot.

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:07.750
So this way we ensured that at
the root of all of our ETLs,

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:10.360
the data matched exactly.

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:12.640
So we ran in parallel
in the DC, made sure

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.710
that we could reconcile
all of the data.

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:16.540
Nothing changed.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:20.740
Again, we didn't violate
our SLOs or SLAs.

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:23.050
And then finally, we
flipped the switch again.

00:31:23.050 --> 00:31:29.640
So until this point, Presto and
Redshift, and all of our data

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:33.030
that was consumed
by our customers

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:37.350
was running from data
that came from our DC.

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:40.470
So we always wanted to
maintain and make sure that we

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:42.660
had a single source of truth.

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:47.970
We didn't want to have some data
in DC and some data in the GCP

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:51.040
and then have to synchronize.

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:56.260
So basically at one point,
we just started to flip.

00:31:56.260 --> 00:31:57.820
We flipped the
switch and started

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:03.970
doing reporting from our--

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:09.070
or sorry, we started loading
data from GCP instead of the DC

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:12.250
and slowly moved all
of our data to have

00:32:12.250 --> 00:32:18.080
the single source of truth in
GCP before we shut down the DC.

00:32:18.080 --> 00:32:20.060
So that was the bulk
of our migration.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.400
Again, these are the logical
pieces that we moved.

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:28.610
There was dozens of
services involved in them.

00:32:31.150 --> 00:32:33.830
And so here's a brief timeline.

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:39.800
So in Q4 2016, David
gave the mandate

00:32:39.800 --> 00:32:46.700
that by Q4 all data processing
needed to be in GCP.

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:50.580
Then 2017 was pretty
much all entirely

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:52.340
eaten up by contract
negotiations

00:32:52.340 --> 00:32:53.580
and working with Google.

00:32:53.580 --> 00:32:57.080
And this is a piece
that I hadn't factored

00:32:57.080 --> 00:32:59.270
into my estimates when
I was building out

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:03.500
capacity and budgeting
for our data center needs

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:04.970
for the data warehouse.

00:33:04.970 --> 00:33:07.640
I assumed that we'd have
the contract signed,

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:09.530
and we'd be able to
get going, and we

00:33:09.530 --> 00:33:12.340
wouldn't need to scale as much.

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:13.770
So it was a bit of a scramble.

00:33:13.770 --> 00:33:17.675
And it was this
game of not wanting

00:33:17.675 --> 00:33:19.800
to invest in the hardware
that we knew we were only

00:33:19.800 --> 00:33:23.100
going to use for a handful of
months versus actually making

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:24.960
sure that we didn't
violate SLOs.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:29.180
So it got a little
tight near the end.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:39.630
So by April 26, we managed to
run all production out of GCP

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:45.120
without any SLO violations
or data disruptions.

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:52.170
And as of July 1, we shut
down the data center.

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:57.120
So one of the things that
you need to think about

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:59.490
is really what are
you optimizing for?

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:01.530
Is it cost, or is it
time to migration?

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:02.920
For us, it was
time to migration.

00:34:02.920 --> 00:34:04.650
We knew that we were
going to potentially

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:06.810
be inefficient with cost.

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:07.990
Our executives knew that.

00:34:07.990 --> 00:34:10.980
And they were OK because
maintaining two legacy

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:11.739
systems--

00:34:11.739 --> 00:34:15.969
or sorry, maintaining
two operational systems

00:34:15.969 --> 00:34:19.303
and also having to renegotiate
DC contracts if we slipped

00:34:19.303 --> 00:34:21.219
was going to be much
more expensive and harder

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:27.010
to do for any longer than
we absolutely needed to.

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:29.929
The other part is really
using the right tools.

00:34:29.929 --> 00:34:36.280
So for us, lean on managed
services as much as possible.

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:39.010
If you can shift operational
burden away from your team

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:41.951
and onto Google, that's a win.

00:34:41.951 --> 00:34:43.909
But you also need to
understand the trade-offs.

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:46.730
For us, we wanted to shift the
operational burden of running

00:34:46.730 --> 00:34:50.330
our YARN cluster to Google but
we couldn't because of these

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:51.739
boot up times.

00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:55.239
And we knew that Dataproc
was working on this.

00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:59.547
But at the point when we
needed to make the decision,

00:34:59.547 --> 00:35:00.380
it wasn't ready yet.

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:03.320
And we weren't ready
to bet all of our ETLs

00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:06.350
on a product that was in alpha
yet-- or a feature, sorry,

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:09.110
that wasn't in alpha yet.

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:12.506
So this is picking the
appropriate path forward.

00:35:12.506 --> 00:35:13.880
Even if you know
it's suboptimal,

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:16.640
and it might raise the
operational burden,

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:18.410
you pick the path
forward that you

00:35:18.410 --> 00:35:21.950
need to make sure that you
can optimize towards what

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:22.813
you're looking for.

00:35:27.070 --> 00:35:29.430
And then for the migration
plan, really for us

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:31.840
it was trying to iterate
as quickly as possible.

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:35.550
So we wanted to make
sure that we were never

00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:38.250
a bottleneck for our
upstream consumers,

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:40.710
and that we could fire
these tracer bullets as

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:43.560
quickly as possible, and
correct any issues that we saw.

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:49.019
So we really wanted to
make sure that we were fast

00:35:49.019 --> 00:35:51.060
and that if something
broke, we could mitigate it

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:53.910
quickly, and then try
and push the system

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:58.590
and figure out any edge
cases as fast as possible.

00:35:58.590 --> 00:36:03.182
And then for us, too, it was
identifying the logical pieces

00:36:03.182 --> 00:36:04.140
that we wanted to move.

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:06.000
For us, in that
dependency graph,

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:10.920
it didn't make sense to try
to more one piece at a time.

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:12.720
The interdependencies
were too complicated.

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:15.190
And it would've slowed us down.

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:17.880
And so what we ended
up doing was, again,

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:23.250
slicing everything vertically
and managing to just move it

00:36:23.250 --> 00:36:27.090
in bulk over and being
able to isolate it

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:29.610
to a single team that
would work on moving

00:36:29.610 --> 00:36:34.150
their piece once the
infrastructure was in place.

00:36:34.150 --> 00:36:35.080
So, that's it.

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:35.920
Thank you.

00:36:35.920 --> 00:36:38.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

